<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">British occupation of Saint-Domingue</h1><p class="st-meta">1793 CE – 1798 CE · capital Port-au-Prince</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's costly five-year attempt to seize revolutionary Saint-Domingue, which cost it tens of thousands of soldiers, mostly to yellow fever.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Royalist French planters, alarmed by the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, invited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to take over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> after war broke out with revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. British troops from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremie">Jérémie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mole-saint-nicolas">Môle Saint-Nicolas</a> in September 1793 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> in June 1794.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The occupiers governed the coastal towns they held in alliance with planters and with restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, relying heavily on locally raised black and coloured troops. They never controlled the interior. Yellow fever and malaria devastated the reinforcements sent from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, now fighting for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fifth-republic">French Republic</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">André Rigaud</a> pressed them from the north and south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With losses mounting, Brigadier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-maitland">Thomas Maitland</a> negotiated an evacuation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint</a> and withdrew the last British forces in 1798, also reaching a secret trade agreement. The failure helped Toussaint consolidate power and strengthened the path to Haitian independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1793 CE – 1798 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.38%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1793 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port-au-Prince</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Maitland, Adam Williamson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Saint-Domingue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=british-occupation-of-saint-domingue" aria-label="British occupation of Saint-Domingue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2721 1490 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British occupation of Saint-Domingue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3041 1746L4997 651M3041 1746L3044 1726"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M3044 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3041 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3041 1746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port-au-Prince. <a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=british-occupation-of-saint-domingue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
